solar systems follow a pretty generic  recipe to begin combine hydrogen helium  oxygen and carbon into a cloud of gas  and dust sprinkle with magnesium iron  and silicon spin it until the cloud  forms star and a planetary disk wait 10  million years or until lumps form in the  disk and voila you've baked a solar  system the planets we found orbiting  other stars are very different from the  ones in our own solar system  so the recipes for cooking up an  exoplanet are probably pretty different  from those for baking in Earth ok so  you've got a star it contains a list of  all the chemical ingredients that a  planet has to work with those  proportions vary from planet to planet  and astronomers are still trying to  figure out what factors produce a rocky  planet versus a gassy water mixing  matters - some planetary discs are more  turbulent than others and where  different elements end up in the mix  determines whether or not they end up in  the planet when a planet forms also  makes a big difference gas giants need a  lot of prep time they have to form a  rocky core before they can start  collecting vapors so they start baking  soon after their star forms small rocky  planets will start later how far a  planet forms in relation to its star  complicates planetary recipes small  planets that form too close to a star  can lose their atmospheres to energetic  winds from the star sometimes big  planets may arise of the system's edges  but finish cooking closer to their star  later on that's one way to make oddballs  like hot versions of Jupiter or Neptune  scientists are studying exactly how  these variables influence planet  formation in other systems and NASA's  latest planet hunting satellite tests  could reveal clues to exotic exoplanet  compositions meanwhile researchers are  cooking up experimental planets in the  lab to try to understand how other  systems formed so one day faking an  exoplanet will be a piece of cake  [Music]  [Music]
 

